### How does it work?

```java
public abstract class Account {
    private final String accountId;
    private final String holderName;

    // Abstract class constructor – called via super() from subclasses
    protected Account(String accountId, String holderName) {
        this.accountId = accountId;
        this.holderName = holderName;
        System.out.println("Account initialized: " + accountId);
    }

    public abstract BigDecimal calculateInterest();

    public String getAccountId() { return accountId; }
}

public class SavingsAccount extends Account {
    private double rate;

    public SavingsAccount(String accountId, String holderName, double rate) {
        super(accountId, holderName);  // calls abstract class constructor
        this.rate = rate;
    }

    @Override
    public BigDecimal calculateInterest() {
        return new BigDecimal(rate / 100);
    }
}

// new Account("SAV2001", "Rajesh");  // COMPILE ERROR: Account is abstract
SavingsAccount sa = new SavingsAccount("SAV2001", "Rajesh", 4.0);  // OK
// Output: Account initialized: SAV2001
```

### Why would an abstract class need a constructor?

- **Initialize common fields.** accountId, holderName, createdAt – shared by all account types.
- **Validate inputs.** Ensure accountId is not null, holderName is not empty.
- **Enforce invariants.** Every account must have a valid ID, regardless of the subclass type.

```java
protected Account(String accountId, String holderName) {
    this.accountId = Objects.requireNonNull(accountId, "accountId must not be null");
    if (holderName == null || holderName.isBlank()) {
        throw new IllegalArgumentException("holderName must not be blank");
    }
    this.holderName = holderName;
}
```

No subclass can bypass this validation because they all must call super().

### What about interfaces?

Interfaces cannot have constructors. Only abstract classes can.

| Feature | Abstract class | Interface |
|---|---|---|
| Constructor | Yes | No |
| Instance fields | Yes | No (only static final constants) |
| Instantiate directly | No | No |
| Multiple inheritance | No (single extends) | Yes (multiple implements) |

### What are common mistakes?

- **Trying to instantiate an abstract class.** `new Account(...)` does not compile.
- **Making the constructor public.** Use `protected` since only subclasses should call it.
- **Forgetting super() in the subclass.** If the abstract class has no no-arg constructor, the subclass must explicitly call super(args).

### What should a senior engineer know?

- **Template method pattern** uses abstract class constructors: the abstract class defines the skeleton (including field initialization in the constructor), and subclasses fill in the abstract methods.
- **Abstract class with all-concrete methods** is legal. It just prevents direct instantiation. Use this when you want to force subclassing.
- **Sealed abstract classes (Java 17+)** restrict which classes can extend them: `public sealed abstract class Account permits SavingsAccount, CurrentAccount {}`.
